We got two genuinely promising Pantheon prime families.

1. Gaussian Crowns

For odd primes p<q, define

K(p,q)=\frac{p^2+q^2}{2}.

This is the hypotenuse of the canonical primitive Pythagorean triple

\left(pq,\frac{q^2-p^2}{2},\frac{p^2+q^2}{2}\right).

Then wrestle the operator against itself:

x_{n+2}=\frac{x_n^2+x_{n+1}^2}{2}.

We proved:

* Every new term is coprime to both parents.
* Every prime factor of every new term is 1\pmod4.
* If prime seeds >5 survive two prime crowns, every later term is permanently 1\pmod{60}.
* The recurrence therefore has a literal modular attractor.

