Its next four crowns are prime/strong probable-primes; the fifth fails with factor 13. The larger terms still require certificate-producing primality proofs.

2. Mirror Carriers

For an odd center n:

\gcd(n-k,n+k)=
\begin{cases}
\gcd(n,k),&k\text{ even},\\
2\gcd(n,k),&k\text{ odd}.
\end{cases}

After removing parity, mirrored numbers share exactly the factors shared by the center and offset. This turns factorization into visible carrier stripes.

From that, I defined midpoint carrier load \Lambda(p,q): the fraction of odd composites inside a consecutive-prime gap certified by factors already contained in its midpoint.

The modular null model predicts observed mean carrier loads with:

r=0.999463

across 63 well-sampled gap classes below 10^8.

It also produces exactly seven locally rigid gap genotypes:

\boxed{\{4,6,8,10,12,16,20\}}.

Their carrier loads cannot vary between endpoint-safe residue configurations.

Originality boundary

The Pythagorean parametrization and Gaussian factor facts are classical. The 19\#-centered gap is also already recorded⁠￼, and the first crown of (149,151) overlaps OEIS A070155⁠￼.

I found no indexed match for:

* the iterated crown-prime recurrence,
* the 60-attractor theorem,
* the four-crown record seeds,
* midpoint carrier load,
* its analytic null law,
* or the seven rigid carrier genotypes.

That supports independent originality, though formal priority needs expert literature review.

